  "metric_definitions": {
    "wape": "sum(|actual - pred|) / sum(|actual|) * 100% (volume-weighted percentage error)",
    "rmse": "sqrt(mean((actual - pred)^2)) in unit quantities",
    "mae": "mean(|actual - pred|) in unit quantities",
    "r2": "1 - SS_res / SS_tot (coefficient of determination)",
    "bias": "sum(pred - actual) / sum(|actual|) * 100% (positive = overforecasting, negative = underforecasting)",
    "mase": "mae_model / mean(|diff(y_train)|) (relative to training naive random walk)"
  },
  "investigation_wape_and_r2": {
    "topic": "Large Saved WAPE and Negative R2 Investigation",
    "root_cause_analysis": "1. Granularity & Intermittency: Product SKU-level demand contains sparse zeros, low volumes, and intermittent sales, where small unit discrepancies (e.g., predicting 3 units when actual is 0 or 1) yield massive percentage errors. 2. Variance Shift vs Mean: In time-series splits, if an item experiences an out-of-sample level drop, global regression models predicting past historical means suffer SS_res > SS_tot, leading to negative R2. 3. Category Aggregation Effect: At the category level, demand is aggregated across SKUs, smoothing intermittency. Category validation WAPE drops to 33.54% with strong positive R2 (0.3169). 4. Mitigation: Hierarchical models (ProductProfileForecaster, Croston, and regularized trees like LightGBM) prevent negative predictions and ground SKU projections in per-product velocity profiles."
  },
